---
import Post from '../../layouts/Post.astro';
import { posts } from '../../blog';

const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === 'early-insights-model-routing')!;

const dek =
  'Spending on coding agents is growing fast, in organizations and among individual developers, and most of it goes to the top of the menu: most Claude Code sessions run on a top-tier model at high effort. How much does that cost? To find out, we built a replay benchmark on real interactive sessions: we replayed multi-turn Claude Code sessions from their original commits on a grid of models and effort levels, with an emulated user sending the original follow-ups. Had each task run on the cheapest setting that completed it, it would have cost 48 % of what the users’ own choices cost. The simple routing rules we tried recover only about a quarter of that saving, so there’s a lot left on the table for a smarter router. We’re releasing the benchmark so others can work on closing the gap.';

const toc = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'The routing problem' },
  { id: 'benchmark', label: 'The benchmark' },
  { id: 'scoring', label: 'Scoring a router' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'Results' },
  { id: 'learned', label: 'What we learned' },
  { id: 'next', label: 'What comes next' },
  { id: 'get', label: 'Get the benchmark' },
  { id: 'references', label: 'References' },
];
---

<Post post={post} eyebrow="Early research · Fidius" dek={dek} toc={toc}>
  <p>Anyone who has used Claude Code for a while knows the menu: pick a model, pick how hard it should think, and get to work. Even on the small grid we test below, the average cost per task varies 13× from the cheapest setting to the most expensive, so the choice matters. In practice, though, most people pick once and never look back – and they pick near the top. In the public SWE-chat dataset<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>, 84 % of 11,215 Claude Code sessions ran on Opus or Fable, the two top tiers, and 88 % of the 1,051 sessions that log effort ran at high effort or above (<a href="#choices">table below</a>).</p>
  <p>That’s a reasonable choice if you’re about to refactor an authentication module. It’s much less reasonable if all you want is to rename a variable. And the mistake can go the other way too: a setting that’s too weak for the task fails, and you pay twice – once for the failed attempt and once for the redo.</p>
  <p>So how should one pick the setting for each task? This is a routing problem, and routing between language models is not new: FrugalGPT<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup> cascades a query through increasingly expensive models, Hybrid LLM<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup> and RouteLLM<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup> train routers that choose between a strong and a weak model, and RouterBench<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup> lets such routers be compared offline. These all route single prompts. Recent work has started moving to agents: TwinRouterBench<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup> routes each model call of an agent working on SWE-bench tasks, Agent-as-a-Router<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup> routes coding tasks, and Parashar et al.<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup> route each user turn of a multi-turn spreadsheet agent. But the sessions in all of these are built from benchmarks. A real coding session is a conversation with a real user, over a long cached prefix, inside a repository, and the only record of what “done” means is the conversation itself.</p>
  <p>Studying routing here needs ground truth that coding benchmarks such as SWE-bench<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-9">9</a></sup> don’t provide: the same real task, run on every candidate setting from the same starting point and judged the same way. To the best of our knowledge no such benchmark exists for real interactive sessions, so we built one. Most of the difficulty lives in the replay: restoring the repository and its dependencies as they were on the day of the session, playing the user so the model gets its follow-ups at the right moments, and judging the result without an answer key, since a replay can solve the task differently and still be correct.</p>
  <p>This post is an early look at the problem. We replay 43 Claude Code sessions from SWE-chat on every cell of a model × effort grid and use the verdicts to compute the oracle – the cheapest setting that completes each task. The oracle isn’t a router; it cheats by knowing every outcome in advance. But it tells us how much there is to win. We’re sharing the benchmark at this stage because we think routing for coding agents is worth working on together.</p>

  <p>The short version: a perfect router would cost 48 % of what the users’ own choices cost. That’s a ceiling, since a real router doesn’t know in advance which settings will pass, but it’s a high one. The cheapest setting that works is often far below the one people pick – in 87 % of the sessions where the user’s choice passed, a cheaper cell passed too, and in 5 of the 43, Haiku alone was enough.</p>

