  <h2 id="problem">The routing problem</h2>
  <p>We state the problem for any coding agent, then the choices we made for this first experiment.</p>
  <h3>The choice space</h3>
  <p>A router chooses among the models and effort levels of the provider family the user's agent already runs. Any setting is allowed, cheaper or more expensive than the one the user asked for. For Claude Code the full menu is the Haiku, Sonnet, Opus and Fable models, with effort from low up to xhigh and max.</p>
  <p>To keep the cost of this experiment down, its grid has seven cells: Sonnet and Opus at low, medium and high effort, plus Haiku, which has no effort levels in the generations we use. Each session is replayed on the model generation it originally ran on, so no candidate is a model released after the session – which is also why Opus 5.5 and Haiku 5.5 don’t appear here yet. For most sessions that means Haiku 4.5, Sonnet 5 and Opus 5; the three that ran on Opus 4.7 or 4.8 get Sonnet 4.6 and their own Opus. The cell the user’s own choice maps to is the reference every cost is compared with. Users who picked Fable, xhigh or max are compared against Opus at high effort, the closest cell in the grid. That makes our numbers conservative: Fable costs twice what Opus does per token, and xhigh and max think longer than high.</p>
  <h3>When a router can switch</h3>
  <p>Why not route every single call? In principle a router could, but coding agents lean heavily on prompt caching<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-10">10</a></sup>. Every turn re-sends a long prefix – the system prompt, tool definitions, project instructions and the conversation so far – and reading that prefix from cache costs a tenth of fresh input or less. The catch is that a cache belongs to one model, and on most current models to one effort level as well. Switch either mid-session and you throw the cache away and pay for the whole prefix again.</p>
  <p>That cost can be earned back – Parashar et al.<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup> find that a switch down to a cheaper model pays for itself within a couple of calls – but switching is free only where the cache is lost anyway: the start of a session, after the conversation is compacted, after the cache expires, or when the prefix itself changes. In general a router should get a say at each of these points. In this first experiment we look only at the first one – a single decision per session, made before the first call to the model, using only what exists at that point – and leave the others for later.</p>
  <h3>The objective</h3>
  <p>A replay completes the task when it meets every acceptance criterion derived from the original session: what the user asked for in each turn, minus anything they later withdrew (see <a href="#judging">Judging each replay</a>). Among the settings that complete the task, the right one is the cheapest. A setting that fails is never the right one, however cheap. Cost is measured per session at list price, from the tokens the session actually used, including cache reads and writes.</p>
  <h3>The oracle</h3>
  <p>For each session, the oracle is the cheapest cell of the grid whose replay passed. It is the best any router could do on this data, given perfect knowledge of how every setting would perform. Where no cell passed, the oracle keeps the user's choice.</p>

  <h2 id="benchmark">The benchmark: real sessions, replayed</h2>

  <h3>Source sessions</h3>
  <p>SWE-chat (Baumann et al., 2026)<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup> is a public dataset of real coding-agent sessions on public repositories, published by developers who opted in. Across all of its Claude Code sessions, users' choices sit at the top of the menu:</p>
