  <h3>An emulated user for the follow-up turns</h3>
  <p>Every session we selected has at least three user turns, and most of the follow-ups give new instructions rather than answer questions. So the replay needs someone on the other side of the conversation. Simulating the user with a language model is an established idea in agent evaluation – τ-bench<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-11">11</a></sup> is a well-known example – and it is also notoriously hard to get right<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-12">12</a></sup>. We keep our emulated user on a short leash. From each original session we write a turn script: each later message the user sent, together with the condition under which it applies. During a replay, the emulated user sends the next message once its condition holds, answers the model’s questions only with what the original user had actually said, and ends the session when the script is done.</p>

  <h3 id="judging">Judging each replay</h3>
  <p>From the original session we derive the task statement and its acceptance criteria, 520 in all on the usable sessions. Each criterion says where the evidence lives: in the final diff (20 %), in a test or build run that the replay itself must show (18 %), or in the conversation (62 %). That last share is large because many requests live in the conversation itself – “explain why the build breaks”, “ask before touching the migrations” – and there is nowhere else to check them. An LLM judge<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-13">13</a></sup> reads the replay's transcript, the tests it ran with their output, and its final diff, and rules on each criterion; a replay passes when it meets all of them. The original result is shown to the judge as a reference for what a complete answer looks like, not as the answer to copy.</p>
  <p>We do not rely on the repository's test suite alone. Many tasks are not covered by any existing test, and some sessions make no code change at all.</p>

  <h3>How reliable the labels are</h3>
  <p>To measure the judge's own noise, we judged 14 replays from two sessions ten times each: a single verdict disagreed with the ten-vote majority in 2 of 140 judgements (1.4 %), and a majority of three never did. In the benchmark, every verdict with confidence below 0.7 is judged twice more and the majority decides.</p>

  <h2 id="scoring">Scoring a router on this benchmark</h2>
  <p>A router submitted to the benchmark sees one session-start record per session: what the harness has before the first call to the model. It returns one cell of that session's grid.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Relative cost</td><td>Total cost of the chosen cells divided by the total cost of the users' choices (100 %). A chosen cell that fails is charged its own cost plus the cost of the user's choice, because the user would redo the work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Share of the oracle</td><td>The router's saving divided by the oracle's saving, on the same sessions. Below zero means it costs more than the users' choices.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tasks lost</td><td>Share of sessions where the chosen cell failed while the user's choice passed.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The benchmark repository has everything needed to score your own router this way.</p>

