  <h2 id="learned">What we learned</h2>
  <h3>Replaying a session is the hard part</h3>
  <p>If there’s one thing we’d tell anyone building a similar benchmark, it’s that the replay is where the effort goes. A real session is a conversation in a complex environment, and the public record of it is thin – a transcript, a start commit, and not much else. From that we have to rebuild the environment, write an emulated user who carries the original user’s intent to a model that may wander off in a different direction, and then label every replay a success or a failure without an answer key, since a different route to the same result is still a success. Every label in the benchmark depends on getting all three right.</p>
  <h3>The right model depends on the task</h3>
  <p>It’s tempting to think of routing as a downgrade rule: send the easy stuff to a cheaper model and be done. The downgrade part is right – in 37 of 43 sessions something cheaper than the user’s choice would have worked. The hard part is knowing <em>how far</em> down. The cheapest working setting is spread across the whole grid, from Haiku all the way to Opus at high effort (Figure 2), and every fixed rule we tried either goes too far and loses tasks, or not far enough and saves little. The best of them reaches 24 % of the oracle’s saving. A router has to answer a per-task question: how much model does <em>this</em> task need?</p>

  <h2 id="next">What comes next</h2>
  <p>This is only our first routing experiment: 43 sessions and a single decision at the start of each one. Next we want more sessions, and the other points where a router can switch – after compaction, or after the cache expires – when it gets a second chance with much more information about the task. The newest models open one more: on Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5 and Haiku 5.5, changing effort no longer throws away the cache<sup class="fn"><a href="#ref-10">10</a></sup>, so effort can be routed turn by turn. SWE-chat has Codex sessions too, so we can check whether the picture holds for other agents. And the big one: how much of the oracle’s saving can a router that sees only the start of a session actually recover?</p>

